<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khusrau Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1157">1157</a>–<a href="/years/1160">1160</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Khusrau Shah of Ghazni</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khusrau Shah was a sultan of the Ghaznavid dynasty and son of Bahram Shah, reigning from 1157 to 1160. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> had been sacked by the Ghurids and was then lost to Oghuz <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a>, so he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> over the remaining Ghaznavid lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. He was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khusrau-malik">Khusrau Malik</a>, the last of the dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khusrau-shah.jpg" alt="Khusrau Shah" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khusrau_Shah_of_the_Justanids.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
